 **Parameters**
-*   **Stepsize Management:** Lower and upper limits are required, relating to the smoothness of the data set [S1].
-*   **Direction Accuracy:** Depends on the wanted tracking accuracy and the order of tensors, because higher orders can produce many maxima that are close together [S1].
-
-**Pitfalls**
-*   **Degenerated Points:** Tracking stops if the stepsize becomes too small, indicating a degenerated point where the Hessian determinant is zero or maxima vanish [S1].
-*   **Multiple Directions:** If multiple directions are close to the current direction during tracking, the point is appended to the set of degenerated points and tracking stops [S1].
-*   **Scalar Fields:** Pure scalar components ($a_{00}$) make tracking impossible and are treated as degenerated [S1].
+*   **b-factor:** Diffusion weighting factor, assumed constant [S1, book].
+*   **Step size limits:** Lower and upper limits related to data smoothness [S2].
+*   **Direction accuracy:** Depends on tracking accuracy and tensor order (higher orders produce closer maxima) [S2].
+*   **Anisotropy Threshold (for switching variant):** 0.2–0.3 depending on SNR [S2].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Degenerate Points:** Positions where the Hessian determinant is zero. Lines are not uniquely defined. Tracking stops or branches [S1, book][S2].
+*   **Scalar Fields:** Pure order zero tensors ($a_{00}$) make tracking impossible [S2].
+*   **Multiple Directions:** If multiple directions are close to the current direction during tracking, the point is considered degenerate and tracking stops [S2].
+*   **Precision:** Higher-order tensors can produce many maxima close together, requiring high direction accuracy [S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### HOT- Lines Fiber Tracking with Anisotropy-Based Switching
-A fiber tracking algorithm that improves speed by using simple second-order tensor tracking in areas with one dominant fiber direction, and switching to higher-order tensor analysis when anisotropy is low or direction deviation is large [S1]. It loops through the grid, computing eigenvectors and anisotropy. If anisotropy is below a threshold (typically 0.2–0.3, depending on SNR) or direction deviation is too large, it processes the next step with higher-order lines; otherwise, it accepts the step using second-order tensor lines [S1]. This leverages the similarity of HOT Lines with second-order tensor lines to optimize performance in regions with single dominant directions [S1].
+A variant that improves speed by using simple second-order tensor tracking in areas with one dominant fiber direction and switching to higher-order tensor analysis when anisotropy is low or direction deviation is large [S2]. It monitors local anisotropy measurements and orientation change. If anisotropy is below a threshold (typically 0.2–0.3) or direction deviation is too large, it processes the next step with higher-order lines; otherwise, it accepts the second-order step [S2]. This leverages the similarity of HOT-lines with second-order tensor lines in dominant direction areas [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The method relies on spherical harmonic expansions. For symmetric tensors of even order $l$, the representation requires $(l+1)(l+2)/2$ real coefficients [S1]. Analytical calculation of gradients is possible, improving speed [S1].
-*   **Performance:** The method is designed for efficiency, particularly when using spherical harmonic representations. It is faster than Q-Space or Q-Ball imaging which compute the Orientation Distribution Function (ODF) directly, avoiding higher data acquisition times [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles complex fiber configurations (crossings, kissings, T/Y-junctions) that second-order tensors fail to resolve. Unifies streamlines and tensor lines.
-*   **Limitations:** Degenerated points (scalar components, isotropic regions) cause tracking to stop. Higher-order tensors can produce many close maxima, requiring careful direction accuracy tuning [S1].
-*   **Use Cases:** Diffusion MRI tractography, visualization of stress fields, and other applications requiring tracking in arbitrary-order tensor fields [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Tracking is $O(N \cdot S)$ where $N$ is the number of seeds and $S$ is the average number of steps. Finding maxima on the sphere is a local optimization problem per step. The anisotropy-based switching variant reduces time in coherent regions by using faster second-order eigenvector extraction [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for storing the tensor field, where $V$ is the number of voxels. Each voxel stores $(l+1)(l+2)/2$ coefficients for order $l$ [S2].
+*   **Performance:** Applied to datasets of size $128 \times 128 \times 44$ with voxel size $1.7 \times 1.7 \times 3.0$ mm$^3$ [S1, book]. Uses 36 gradient directions [S1, book].
+*   **Strengths:** Detects crossings locally without reducing resolution; preserves high angular resolution; generalizes streamlines and tensor lines [S1, book][S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Computationally more expensive than second-order tensor lines; sensitive to noise and degenerate points; requires careful step-size control [S1, book][S2].
+*   **When to use:** When visualizing fiber crossings in diffusion MRI data where second-order tensors fail (e.g., corpus callosum/pyramidal tract crossing) [S1, book].
+*   **When not to use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and crossings are rare; or when data is purely scalar/isotropic [S2].
 
 ## Background
-HOT Lines were introduced to address the limitations of second-order tensor visualization in medical imaging, specifically in diffusion MRI where fiber crossings are common [S1]. The method generalizes tensor lines to arbitrary orders using spherical harmonics, building on the mathematical properties of symmetric tensors and spherical harmonic expansions [S1]. It was developed to provide a unified framework for tracking lines in vector fields (streamlines) and higher-order tensor fields, with specific applications in understanding the connectivity and function of the human brain [S1].
+HOT-lines were introduced by Hlawitschka and Scheuermann [S1, book][S2]. The method generalizes streamlines (first-order tensors) and tensor lines (second-order tensors) to arbitrary even-order symmetric tensors. It was applied to MRI data to solve the tractography problem, specifically to address the limitation of second-order tensors in representing fiber crossings. The approach uses spherical harmonic representation for efficiency and relies on the implicit function theorem to ensure $C^1$ continuity of the tracked lines [S1, book][S2].
 
